component SigilLanguage {
  goal {
    Define the versioned source and interpretation contract for Sigil documents.

    Give deterministic tools one structural model for components, contracts,
    Facets, Tags, explicit selections, Inline Links, and recoverable diagnostics.
  }

  interface {
    LanguageVersion {
      sigilVersion = "0.8.0"

      Workspace configuration declares the LanguageVersion that governs
      interpretation.
    }

    SourceDocument {
      Accept strict UTF-8 .sigil source under SourceGrammar and preserve original
      text, physical lines, byte offsets, declarations, Facets, and payloads.

      Return structural diagnostics and deterministic partial results according to
      SourceRecovery; recovery retains invalidity and completeness evidence.

      Diagnostic message prose is explanatory; code, stage, severity, source and
      related locations, ordering, retained evidence, and suppression follow the
      normative reference.
    }

    SourceGrammar {
      The [language reference](sigil-reference.md) and
      [EBNF grammar](sigil.ebnf) jointly govern all 0.8 syntax, lexical algorithms,
      ownership, resolution, formatting, interpretation, and required diagnostics.

      The [migration guide](migrating-to-0.8.md) explains replacement of earlier
      source notation; it does not define another grammar or implemented support.
    }

    SourceRecovery {
      Preserve partial evidence without treating recovered nodes as valid authored
      declarations. Apply the reference's diagnostic catalog, ordering, recovery,
      related-location, and suppression rules.

      Invalid UTF-8 stops parsing that source; independent valid sources remain
      available. Incomplete regions cannot prove absent requirements or references.
    }

    ComponentContract {
      A component owns one responsibility and all seven permitted contract kinds
      in one declaration; Goal and Interface each contain at least one Facet.

      Interface exposes operations, data, events, results, failures, and observable
      promises available to dependents. Interpret selected Tags within the owner's
      complete design while retaining each Facet's owner and contract role.
    }

    TagIdentity {
      A Tag is identified by its exact name and component owner. One inline
      definition and any repeated local grouping headings share that identity
      independently of occurrence or section order.

      Grouping remains optional. Direct and grouped Facets may coexist in every
      contract; a component needs no Tags when reusable names add no meaning.

      Every component-owned Tag is selectable regardless of its contract. A local
      heading never acquires an imported identity, and consumer Facets never
      contribute upstream to the provider's design.
    }

    ImportSemantics {
      Select exact Tag names from their named owner in an explicit included .sigil
      source using the reference's workspace-root-relative import rules.

      All components in the importing source see the selected identities. An
      import introduces neither a component namespace nor a re-export or runtime
      call; documentary context may instead use Inline Links.

      Each valid, unambiguous selection requires an eligible bare reference in
      that source. Any contract and Embedded Facet introduction can supply use;
      complete links, payloads, headings, and provider names alone cannot.
    }

    SectionSemantics {
      Goal states purpose, responsibility, and intended outcomes.

      Interface describes offered interactions and observable promises.

      State describes meaningful runtime or domain data, configurations, modes,
      and conditions.

      Logic describes behavior, flows, algorithms, transformations, guards, and
      lifecycle transitions.

      Constraints record binding invariants, prohibitions, bounds, architecture,
      and technology choices.

      Decisions record rationale, scope, assumptions, alternatives, trade-offs,
      consequences, and revisit conditions without turning proposals into policy.

      Cases describe starting situations, actions, and expected observations in
      examples or families; an example does not silently quantify over all inputs.
    }

    FacetDiscipline {
      Each maximal run of adjacent prose lines forms one Facet. Blank lines and
      structural boundaries separate Facets; legal wrapping preserves their
      meaning and does not create or remove Tag references.

      Preserve each Facet's owner, contract role, optional local grouping Tag,
      source range, original lines, and content references.
    }

    EmbeddedContent {
      Introducing prose followed immediately by one fenced payload forms an
      Embedded Facet. Its closing fence ends the Facet, and any following prose
      begins another Facet; each additional payload needs a new introduction.

      Preserve payload bytes, raw lines, delimiter ranges, and relative indentation
      as inert Sigil content. A dedented presentation removes only the opener's
      exact indentation prefix where present.
    }

    InlineLinks {
      Preserve complete Inline Links and images, including labels, destinations,
      optional titles, raw spans, ranges, and enclosing Facet identity.

      Resolve local targets relative to their source directory and retain query
      and fragment meaning. Parsing does not retrieve or adopt linked material;
      containing prose and contract determine its role.

      Required unavailable material leaves interpretation unresolved; retain the
      authored link and the provenance of material actually read separately.
    }

    FormattingStyle {
      Ordinary prose follows the reference's 79-content-character limit and
      exemptions for structural lines, fences, payloads, and link destinations.

      Wrapping preserves exact Tag names, reference identities, Facet boundaries,
      complete links, and payloads, and never turns prose into structural syntax.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    LanguageVersion {
      LanguageVersion is the sole owner of the Sigil language and
      workspace-configuration version literal.

      Tools interpret only the exact configured LanguageVersion they support and
      reject earlier or later version literals without applying semantic-version
      ranges.

      During pre-production 0.y.z development, breaking interpretation changes
      increment the minor version and reset the patch version.

      After 1.0.0, breaking interpretation changes require a new major version.

      Before and after 1.0.0, compatible syntax or semantic functionality increments
      the minor version and resets patch; an interpretation-preserving behavioral
      clarification or defect correction increments patch; a clarification that
      changes any accepted result follows the applicable breaking-change rule;
      interpretation-neutral editorial changes do not change LanguageVersion.
    }

    SourceDocument {
      Preserve a permitted initial BOM and LF, CRLF, or lone CR in original byte
      offsets. Reject NUL, invalid UTF-8, and invalid scalar string inputs without
      replacement decoding, Unicode normalization, or case folding.

      Source ranges use half-open UTF-8 byte offsets. Derive optional one-based
      scalar coordinates and host UTF-16 coordinates from the same snapshot;
      neither character coordinate is a byte offset.
    }

    ComponentContract {
      Component names are workspace-unique and case-sensitive. Duplicate owners
      remain unresolved with all conflicting evidence; source order chooses none.

      Language-level expand and module-index forms are retired. A component may
      govern multiple implementation files while keeping one source declaration.

      Implementation access restrictions remain authored constraints or observable
      promises. Importability grants no runtime access or behavioral equivalence.
    }

    DeclarationMultiplicity {
      Each contract occurs at most once in a component. Duplicate sections retain
      invalid separate evidence; they never merge automatically.

      Repeated inline definitions and duplicate selections are errors. Repeated
      local headings reuse one Tag; collisions preserve every owner and leave the
      affected name unresolved without hiding independent valid identities.
    }

    TagIdentity {
      Match names exactly on original physical prose lines under the reference's
      Unicode word boundaries, connector runs, and longest-overlap algorithm.
      Never normalize internal whitespace, case, or Unicode spelling.

      Structural scanning identifies Facets, payloads, complete links, and inline
      definitions before collective resolution. Complete links and payloads are
      excluded from Tag scanning; introducing prose follows ordinary Tag rules.

      Tag identity supports grouping, navigation, and highlighting but does not
      establish behavioral equality or a source-anchor mapping by itself.
    }

    ImportSemantics {
      Keep independently resolved selections after another path, provider, or Tag
      fails. Allowed cycles terminate through visited identities and cannot
      synthesize a missing definition or expose unselected vocabulary.

      Only explicit included .sigil paths select providers. The ordinary filename
      _module.sigil has no directory-index, export, or re-export behavior.
    }

    SectionSemantics {
      Optional contracts and grouping are not completeness requirements. State
      need not expose storage layout unless the layout itself carries meaning.

      Decisions remain optional free-form rationale. They record concise chosen
      conclusions and supporting reasons, excluding prompts, session transcripts,
      generated approval records, and hidden reasoning.

      Decision scope states where a choice applies and its important exclusions.
      A binding outcome remains in Constraints; Decisions explains the choice.
    }

    FormattingStyle {
      An unformattable source or invalid recovered source yields diagnostics and
      no writable formatting result. Recovery cannot repair validity silently.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    LanguageVersion {
      Decision: Require an explicit configured LanguageVersion and use semantic
      version increments to signal interpretation compatibility.

      Scope: Governs Sigil source and workspace-configuration interpretation;
      excludes CLI, core, LSP, skill, and editor artifact versions.

      Trade-offs: Pre-production breaking changes consume minor versions instead of
      treating every 0.y.z change as equivalent.

      Consequences: Tools reject every non-exact configured version and incompatible
      changes require an explicit migration.

      Revisit when: LanguageVersion reaches 1.0.0 or the compatibility policy
      changes.
    }

    SourceDocument {
      Decision: Bind language behavior to the shared reference and EBNF rather than
      duplicate their grammar and diagnostic tables in another contract.

      Scope: Governs language interpretation; excludes implementation conformance
      and the artifact versions of tools adopting the language.

      Consequences: Compact authored contracts retain domain ownership while one
      normative specification settles lexical and recovery details.
    }

    TagIdentity {
      Decision: Separate exact owner-scoped identity from optional grouping and
      collect introductions before resolving eligible prose references.

      Scope: Governs Tags, occurrences, and import selections; glossary identity
      and implementation source anchors retain their independent contracts.

      Consequences: Consumer reuse preserves provider identity and local Facet
      ownership without visibility layers, expansion attachment, or re-export.
    }

    SemanticParagraphs {
      Decision: Make each blank-line-delimited prose paragraph one Facet
      whose legal physical wrapping may change without changing identity
      or the exact Tag references recognized on source lines.

      Scope: Governs free-form content in every component contract;
      excludes structural forms and attached literal-body content.

      Trade-offs: Adjacent physical lines merge semantically and migration from
      line-based interpretation requires review.

      Design issues addressed: A deterministic formatter cannot wrap prose safely
      when every physical line creates a different Facet.

      Discarded alternatives: Preserving physical-line identity and adding
      continuation punctuation were rejected.

      Consequences: Formatting can reflow prose while preserving Tag references and
      keeping blank lines as the explicit separator between ideas.
    }

    LiteralFencedBlocks {
      Decision: Attach a Markdown-shaped typed backtick fence directly to preceding
      prose and preserve its body as uninterpreted literal content.

      Scope: Governs examples, configuration, code, data, diagrams, and other
      multiline literal content inside sections and local grouping blocks.

      Trade-offs: Standalone or blank-line-detached fences are invalid and literal
      content is unavailable to semantic reference and glossary scanning.

      Design issues addressed: Multiline code and data currently collide with
      structural
      recognition, Facet separation, and line-width formatting.

      Discarded alternatives: Treating each literal line as prose, interpreting
      literal braces, and inventing format-specific section syntax were rejected.

      Consequences: Authors can embed large typed blocks without changing semantic
      separators or creating accidental references.
    }

    DeterministicFormatting {
      Decision: Canonically wrap ordinary prose to 79 content characters excluding
      leading indentation while preserving semantic identity and literal bodies.

      Scope: Governs physical presentation of Sigil prose; excludes structural lines,
      fence delimiters, literal bodies, and link destinations.

      Trade-offs: An indivisible token longer than the limit remains unchanged,
      produces error SIGIL_UNFORMATTABLE_LINE over its complete physical line, and
      prevents a writable formatting result.

      Design issues addressed: Unbounded prose lines reduce side-by-side review
      readability and discourage concrete configuration and code examples.

      Consequences: Deterministic formatting is reviewable, idempotent, and
      independent from nesting depth.
    }

    StateData {
      Decision: Let state describe meaningful runtime or domain data alongside
      configurations, modes, and conditions.

      Scope: Governs data that exists or changes as part of component semantics;
      excludes implementation storage layout unless that layout carries domain
      meaning.

      Trade-offs: Authors must distinguish meaningful data from transient algorithm
      variables and persistence mechanics.

      Design issues addressed: Treating state as modes and conditions alone omits
      data-bearing configurations that implementations and dependents must
      understand.

      Discarded alternatives: Putting all data in interface or treating every stored
      field as state was rejected because visibility and storage do not determine
      semantic ownership.

      Consequences: State can capture data values and structures that materially
      shape behavior without exposing database or memory layout by default.
    }

    SectionSemantics {
      Decision: Keep decisions optional and free-form while using them for concise
      human-reviewed rationale separate from binding constraints.

      Scope: Governs decisions sections in component declarations; excludes generated
      receipts, approval records, and binding authority.

      Trade-offs: Deterministic tools cannot guarantee that every rationale record is
      complete.

      Discarded alternatives: Constraints-only rationale, mandatory decision schemas,
      raw transcripts, and generated receipts as the sole record were rejected.

      Consequences: Binding outcomes remain in constraints and their rationale stays
      colocated with the governing component.

      Revisit when: Free-form records drift or reliable agent use requires a reviewed
      schema.
    }

    ImportResolution {
      Decision: Resolve every import from the selected workspace root through one
      lexical normalization and exact included-file selection rule.

      Scope: Governs import target identity and workspace containment; excludes
      workspace discovery and operating-system symbolic-link identity.

      Trade-offs: Source-relative imports are unavailable and traversal outside the
      selected workspace remains unresolved even when a filesystem target exists.

      Discarded alternatives: Declaring-source-relative lookup, implicit extension
      search, platform-dependent case folding, and multiple candidate targets were
      rejected.

      Consequences: Equivalent lexical paths produce one deterministic dependency
      edge, and selections resolve only against the named component owner.
    }
  }

  cases {
    SourceDocument {
      A minimal component containing nonempty Goal and Interface is valid without
      grouping or Tags. Empty required content is invalid.

      In A😀B, B starts at scalar column three, UTF-16 column four, and byte offset
      five when the line begins at byte zero; host positions translate explicitly.

      A recovered unclosed fence retains the remaining source as payload and marks
      enclosing completeness unknown instead of scanning apparent declarations.
    }

    TagIdentity {
      One inline definition and repeated headings reuse one owner-scoped Tag in
      any section order. Two inline definitions of that exact name are invalid.

      Imported search results matches only that exact single-line spelling;
      repeated spaces or a physical newline do not become the same reference.

      Attaching a payload does not change Tag recognition in unchanged introducing
      prose. Complete link labels and payload text cannot supply import use.
    }

    ImportSemantics {
      A missing selected Tag leaves other independent selections available. A valid
      mutual import is not invalid solely because it forms a cycle.

      Selecting two originating Tags with the same accessible name leaves that
      name unresolved; a local heading with that spelling is also a collision.
    }
  }
}

component SigilWorkspaceConfig {
  goal {
    Define the configuration contract that selects and identifies one Sigil
    workspace.

    Keep workspace boundaries and source discovery independent from package
    manifests and ordinary source summaries.
  }

  interface {
    ConfigFile {
      filename: .sigil/config.json

      location: workspace root
    }

    ConfiguredLanguageVersion {
      sigilVersion: the supported Sigil language version used to interpret the
      workspace
    }

    WorkspaceBoundary {
      workspace.name: non-empty stable workspace identifier

      workspace.members: optional array of unique workspace-relative project-root
      paths
    }

    SourceSelection {
      files.include: non-empty array of workspace-relative POSIX glob strings

      files.exclude?: array of workspace-relative POSIX glob strings
    }

    ToolConfiguration {
      tools?: object of namespaced JSON objects preserved for host integrations
    }
  }

  state {
    ConfiguredLanguageVersion {
      The supported sigilVersion follows the language version in the
      [normative reference](sigil-reference.md).
    }

    SourceSelection {
      default files.include is ["**/*.sigil"]

      default files.exclude is [".git/**", ".deno/**", "node_modules/**", "build/**",
      "coverage/**"]
    }

    ToolConfiguration {
      default tools is {}
    }
  }

  logic {
    WorkspaceBoundary {
      Without an explicit root, discovery walks from the target toward filesystem
      root and selects the nearest eligible ConfigFile.

      A nearer ConfigFile owns the target when every configured parent excludes that
      nearer workspace root.

      A configured parent that includes the nearer workspace root produces a
      multiple-ancestor configuration error.

      With an explicit root, discovery requires ConfigFile directly inside the
      supplied root and does not search elsewhere.

      A nested ConfigFile inside an excluded subtree defines an independent workspace
      and is skipped by the parent.

      A nested ConfigFile inside an included subtree produces an error and its
      subtree is not loaded.
    }

    ConfigValidation {
      Discovery parses and validates ConfigFile before loading any Sigil source.
    }

    SourceSelection {
      File discovery evaluates include globs against normalized workspace-relative
      POSIX paths.

      Exclude matches take precedence over include matches.

      The pattern **/*.sigil matches Sigil files at the workspace root and in nested
      directories.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    ConfigFile {
      ConfigFile is the sole authority for the Sigil workspace boundary.

      The directory containing .sigil/config.json is .sigil; its parent is the
      workspace root and root project location.
    }

    ConfiguredLanguageVersion {
      The configured sigilVersion must equal a language version supported by the
      interpreting tool.
    }

    WorkspaceBoundary {
      Each workspace.members entry declares one additional project root in the same
      workspace.

      Member paths are unique, non-root, workspace-relative directories that cannot
      escape the workspace or overlap.

      A declared member does not contain its own ConfigFile.

      A nested directory with its own ConfigFile is an independent workspace and must
      be excluded from every configured parent.

      Package and repository workspace manifests do not declare Sigil workspace
      members.
    }

    SourceSelection {
      SourceSelection controls source discovery but does not declare workspace
      members or restrict ordinary source summary locations.
    }

    SummaryFiles {
      An ordinary _module.sigil source has no workspace-discovery or membership
      authority.
    }

    LocalToolConfiguration {
      LocalConfigFile is the optional .sigil/local.json developer configuration
      beside ConfigFile.

      LocalConfigFile contains only an optional tools object. Its values recursively
      override matching tools objects from ConfigFile; a local scalar, array, or null
      replaces the matching shared value.

      LocalConfigFile cannot override sigilVersion, workspace, files, or any other
      ConfigFile top-level field. Absence leaves ConfigFile tools unchanged.

      A malformed LocalConfigFile or a local top-level key other than tools makes
      workspace configuration invalid without changing ConfigFile.

      LocalConfigFile is developer-local configuration and is excluded from version
      control by the repository's .gitignore.
    }

    ConfigValidation {
      ConfigFile contains strict UTF-8 JSON.

      Required top-level keys are sigilVersion, workspace, and files.

      Unknown top-level keys are rejected.
    }

    WorkspaceBoundary {
      workspace contains required name and optional members keys and rejects unknown
      keys.

      workspace.members contains unique non-root workspace-relative directory paths
      that do not overlap.

      Package manifests may inform human proposals but do not participate in
      workspace discovery.

      ConfigFile defines workspace roots and members independently of ordinary
      summary source files.

      workspace.members identifies configured summary boundaries for Brownfield
      review without granting or restricting ordinary summary source eligibility.
    }

    SourceSelection {
      files contains required include and optional exclude keys and rejects unknown
      keys.
    }

    ToolConfiguration {
      tools is optional and core preserves namespaced JSON objects without
      interpreting them.
    }

    ConfigurationScope {
      Project descriptions and rationale remain outside ConfigFile.

      Package and release metadata remain in their owning manifests.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    WorkspaceBoundary {
      Decision: Make ConfigFile the sole authority for workspace roots and declared
      members.

      Scope: Governs the workspace root, declared members, and independent nested
      workspaces; excludes ordinary summary sources and GlossaryFile authority.

      Trade-offs: Repositories maintain explicit Sigil configuration in addition to
      package or build manifests.

      Discarded alternatives: Inferring boundaries from package manifests or
      directory structure was rejected because those sources vary by host and
      purpose.

      Consequences: Nested independent workspaces must be excluded explicitly and
      package metadata cannot silently change Sigil membership.

      Revisit when: Another explicit interoperable authority can replace ConfigFile
      without introducing boundary ambiguity.
    }
  }

  cases {
    LocalToolConfiguration {
      A missing LocalConfigFile preserves the ConfigFile tools object.

      A LocalConfigFile can replace one host-local tool setting while retaining all
      other shared tools fields.

      A LocalConfigFile cannot alter workspace membership or Sigil source selection.
    }

    WorkspaceBoundary {
      A target governed by the nearest eligible .sigil/config.json resolves the
      parent of .sigil as its workspace root.

      A target with no ancestor ConfigFile reports SIGIL_CONFIG_NOT_FOUND and loads
      no Sigil files.

      A nested ConfigFile excluded by every configured parent resolves independently.

      A nested ConfigFile still included by a configured parent reports
      SIGIL_NESTED_CONFIG and loads no source.

      An explicit root without a direct ConfigFile reports SIGIL_CONFIG_NOT_FOUND.
    }

    ConfigValidation {
      Malformed JSON reports SIGIL_CONFIG_PARSE and loads no Sigil files.

      Missing, mistyped, empty, or unknown fields report SIGIL_CONFIG_INVALID and
      load no Sigil files.
    }

    ConfiguredLanguageVersion {
      An unsupported sigilVersion reports an error diagnostic and loads no Sigil
      files.
    }

    SourceSelection {
      A matching excluded path is not loaded even when an include pattern also
      matches it.
    }

    WorkspaceBoundary {
      The workspace root and each declared member are configured summary boundaries
      for Brownfield review.

      An undeclared package directory is not a configured summary boundary even when
      it contains a package manifest.
    }
  }
}
